{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Post-merge Windows sweep (run 44232977541 at dd72b461814, job 134562252974): 3 failed, 19627 passed\n\n### Observation\n- `test_xdist_thread_timeout_names_itself_and_the_test` now passes on Windows.\n- The real-worker banner reaches the controller. It reports `+++ Timeout +++ pytest-timeout expired after 600.0s in tests/unit/test_store_commit_maintenance_bd66.py::test_write_survives_foreground_maintenance_repack (xdist worker gw3)`, with stacks pointing into `_fill_loose`'s `git hash-object --stdin-paths`. That makes bd66 a genuine Windows timeout, filed as powderblue-effectual-racerunner.\n- The new `test_xdist_timeout_banner_survives_windows_worker_stderr` failed on Windows. Cause: its sitecustomize runs before execnet in the worker, so on Windows the two redirections stack. The emulation points fd 2 at NUL first, then execnet dups that NUL fd 2 into `sys.stderr`. This is a defect in the test's emulation only. The fix is to apply the emulation only when `os.name != \"nt\"`; on Windows the test then exercises execnet's real redirection, which the original test shows works.\n- `test_unique_cache_writes.py::test_concurrent_cursor_writes_never_report_a_write_error` is a new Windows failure. It is unrelated to the crash guard and is filed as sporting-feudalist-bluet.\n\n### Next\nA follow-up change lands the emulation guard, followed by a re-run of the Windows sweep."},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1794978524601629974,"uuid":"b6d029e2-5e78-4919-aacc-06d34e314026"}